  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms are possible on Sunday.\n\nA potent shortwave by late-summer standards and an associated\narea of low pressure will track across Ontario toward Hudson \nBay on Sunday, with a likely convectively induced disturbance \nand associated weak area of low pressure tracking into the Great\nLakes. Low-level winds will turn out of the south in response to\nthis disturbance, drawing a boundary that is currently stalled\nout to our south northeastward as a warm front. This front is\nexpected to clear much of the area by Sunday evening, advecting\nin a very warm, humid, and highly unstable airmass into the\nregion. However there are some uncertainties with regard to just\nhow far north and east this highly unstable airmass will make\nit. Guidance is in good agreement that locations to the west of\nthe Blue Ridge will destabilize, but there's greater spread in\nsolutions further east.\n\nAn atypically strong low-level jet for late summer will\naccompany this system. Model soundings show large, clockwise\nturning hodographs, with ample streamwise vorticity and SRH at\nlow-levels. When combined with 2000 J/kg of MLCAPE, this raises\nconcerns for severe thunderstorms, with supercells possible.\nDamaging winds should be the primary threat, but a tornado or\ntwo can't be ruled out given the high SRH environment in place.\nAs mentioned before, locations to the west of the Blue Ridge\nappear to have the greatest chance for severe thunderstorms, but\nthere is plenty of time for things to change with two days of\nconvection upstream of the event.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm conditions and daily chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected through Monday, with \ndrier conditions for Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nA typical late summertime pattern will remain in place through\nearly next week, with seasonable temperatures and daily chances\nfor showers and thunderstorms. High temperatures will generally\nbe in the mid 80s to around 90 each day, with overnight lows in\nthe upper 60s to low 70s.\n\nEarly this morning we continue to monitor ongoing thunderstorm \nactivity in the Mississippi/Ohio Valleys. An extensive area of \nthunderstorms has developed to the north of a stationary front \nacross Illinois, Indiana, and western Ohio, with additional \nthunderstorm activity ongoing in West Virginia. Much of this \nactivity is expected to eventually dissipate, but remnant mid- \nupper level disturbances associated with this activity will \nlikely progress into the area later today, eventually sparking \nthe development of showers and thunderstorms. Instability is \nmainly expected to be focused across southwestern portions of \nthe forecast area, with locations to the south and west of the \nPotomac having the greatest chance for a thunderstorm this \nafternoon. A passing shower can't be ruled out anywhere, but \nnortheastern Maryland will have the greatest chance to remain \ndry.\n\nThunderstorm activity is expected to be focused even further \noff toward the southwest on Saturday, with the Central \nShenandoah Valley and Central Virginia having the greatest \nchance for a storm. The background environment both today and \ntomorrow has vertical profiles that are deeply saturated, with \nmodest flow through the mid-levels (around 35 knots of 0-6 km \nshear). DCAPE values will be low given the saturated profiles, \nbut a wet microburst or two can't be ruled out. SPC has \nsouthwestern portions of the forecast area outlooked in a \nMarginal Risk for severe thunderstorms today, with damaging \nwinds being the primary risk. On Saturday the Marginal Risk only\nclips Highland an Augusta Counties.\n\nFollowing a greater severe thunderstorm threat on Sunday, there\nmay be additional thunderstorms across the forecast area on\nMonday, but details remain highly uncertain this far out. Most\nguidance suggests that a cold front will move through later\nMonday into Monday night as high pressure builds to our north.\nTemperatures will change very little behind this front, but\ndrier air will advect in from the north, with dewpoints likely\ndropping into the 50s on Tuesday. This drier air will suppress\nthunderstorm activity, most likely leading to dry conditions on\nboth Tuesday and Wednesday.",
